questions.solution_text:
$x=4$ comes from the factor $(x-4)^{2}$, so its multiplicity is $2$.
An even multiplicity means the curve is tangent to the $x$-axis and turns back.

[tikz]
\begin{tikzpicture}[every node/.style={font=\large}]
\begin{axis}[axis lines=middle, xlabel={$x$}, ylabel={$y$}, xmin=-2, xmax=6.3, ymin=-20.5, ymax=25.5, samples=120, clip=false, width=9cm, height=8cm]
\addplot[smooth, thick, <->, domain=-1.62:5.9] {(x-4)*(x-4)*(x+1)};
\end{axis}
\end{tikzpicture}
[/tikz]

$x=-1$ has multiplicity $2$, which is even, so the curve touches there.
$x=2$ has multiplicity $3$, which is odd and at least $3$, so the curve flattens along the axis and crosses at $x=2$.

[tikz]
\begin{tikzpicture}[every node/.style={font=\large}]
\begin{axis}[axis lines=middle, xlabel={$x$}, ylabel={$y$}, xmin=-2.2, xmax=3.7, ymin=-40.5, ymax=40.5, samples=120, clip=false, width=9cm, height=8cm]
\addplot[smooth, thick, <->, domain=-1.84:3.29] {(x+1)*(x+1)*(x-2)*(x-2)*(x-2)};
\end{axis}
\end{tikzpicture}
[/tikz]

questions.question_text:
The graph of a cubic polynomial is shown. Its only $x$-intercepts are at $x=-3$ and $x=1$. What is the multiplicity of the zero at $x=-3$?

[tikz]
\begin{tikzpicture}[every node/.style={font=\large}]
\begin{axis}[axis lines=middle, xlabel={$x$}, ylabel={$y$}, xmin=-5, xmax=2.1, ymin=-14.5, ymax=14.5, samples=120, clip=false, width=9cm, height=8cm]
\addplot[smooth, thick, <->, domain=-4.57:1.65] {(x+3)*(x+3)*(x-1)};
\end{axis}
\end{tikzpicture}
[/tikz]

The graph of a quartic polynomial is shown, with $x$-intercepts at $x=-2$ and $x=1$ only. What are the multiplicities of these two zeroes?

[tikz]
\begin{tikzpicture}[every node/.style={font=\large}]
\begin{axis}[axis lines=middle, xlabel={$x$}, ylabel={$y$}, xmin=-2.7, xmax=2.8, ymin=-12.5, ymax=12.5, samples=120, clip=false, width=9cm, height=8cm]
\addplot[smooth, thick, <->, domain=-2.32:2.4] {(x-1)*(x-1)*(x-1)*(x+2)};
\end{axis}
\end{tikzpicture}
[/tikz]

The graph of a cubic polynomial is shown. It touches the $x$-axis at $x=2$, cuts it at $x=-1$, and passes through $(0,-8)$. What is its equation?

[tikz]
\begin{tikzpicture}[every node/.style={font=\large}]
\begin{axis}[axis lines=middle, xlabel={$x$}, ylabel={$y$}, xmin=-2.1, xmax=3.9, ymin=-20.5, ymax=20.5, samples=120, clip=false, width=9cm, height=8cm]
\addplot[smooth, thick, <->, domain=-1.71:3.49] {-2*(x-2)*(x-2)*(x+1)};
\end{axis}
\end{tikzpicture}
[/tikz]

The graph shown touches the $x$-axis at $x=-1$ and at $x=2$, and has no other $x$-intercepts. What is the least possible degree of the polynomial?

[tikz]
\begin{tikzpicture}[every node/.style={font=\large}]
\begin{axis}[axis lines=middle, xlabel={$x$}, ylabel={$y$}, xmin=-2.2, xmax=3.2, ymin=-1.5, ymax=8.5, samples=120, clip=false, width=9cm, height=8cm]
\addplot[smooth, thick, <->, domain=-1.77:2.77] {(x+1)*(x+1)*(x-2)*(x-2)};
\end{axis}
\end{tikzpicture}
[/tikz]

A quartic polynomial flattens along the $x$-axis as it crosses at $x=-3$, cuts the $x$-axis at $x=1$, and passes through $(0,54)$. Its graph is shown. What is its equation?

[tikz]
\begin{tikzpicture}[every node/.style={font=\large}]
\begin{axis}[axis lines=middle, xlabel={$x$}, ylabel={$y$}, xmin=-5.2, xmax=1.9, ymin=-70.5, ymax=70.5, samples=120, clip=false, width=9cm, height=8cm]
